    "exp3_shared_strategy_mean_not_merged_recall": 0.125,
    "note": "Exp 3 = LLM on human-written PRs (16 complete, 14:2 merged, base rate 0.875); Exp 4 = LLM on AI-authored PRs (3 complete, 2:1 merged, base rate 0.67). Populations, sample sizes, and context tiers all differ, so read these as directional, not a controlled comparison. Two things survive the small n: (1) raw accuracy is higher on human code, but in BOTH experiments the baseline strategies barely exceed their always-merge base rate, so the gap is mostly the different base rates, not discriminative power; (2) on human code the shared strategies DO reach the minority class occasionally (few-shot 0.25, role-based 0.125, CoT 0.0 averaged over contexts), whereas on AI-authored code those same three strategies score 0.0 not-merged recall everywhere and only Exp 4's NEW strategies (self-reflection 0.6, multi-turn 0.5) catch it at all -- consistent with guide 4.3's premise that AI-generated code is harder to judge from the diff alone. See central_analysis."
